{"causes": ["The ongoing Fitna of Al-Andalus, a civil war that began in 1009, severely weakened the central authority of the Umayyad Caliphate of Córdoba.", "The increasing military and political influence of Berber mercenary factions, who often dictated the succession of caliphs.", "The ambition and strategic position of the Hammudid family, who controlled key ports like Ceuta and Tangier, providing a base for their ascent."], "effects": ["Ali ibn Hammud's caliphate further destabilized the central authority of Córdoba, accelerating the fragmentation of Al-Andalus into independent Taifa kingdoms.", "His brief rule established a precedent for non-Umayyad claimants to the caliphate, fundamentally altering the traditional dynastic legitimacy of the institution.", "The Hammudid dynasty, originating from Ali's usurpation, subsequently established independent Taifa kingdoms in regions like Málaga and Algeciras."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "sulayman-ibn-al-hakam", "sourceName": "Sulayman ibn al-Hakam", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "ali-ibn-hammud-al-nasir", "targetName": "Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir", "context": "Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir succeeded Sulayman ibn al-Hakam as Caliph of Córdoba in 1016 after defeating him in battle."}, {"sourceSlug": "ali-ibn-hammud-al-nasir", "sourceName": "Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "taifa-kingdoms", "targetName": "Taifa Kingdoms", "context": "Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir's usurpation and non-Umayyad rule contributed significantly to the fragmentation of the Caliphate into Taifa kingdoms after 1018."}, {"sourceSlug": "ali-ibn-hammud-al-nasir", "sourceName": "Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "cordoba-spain", "targetName": "Córdoba, Spain", "context": "Ali ibn Hammud al-Nasir's caliphate was centered in Córdoba, the capital of Al-Andalus, from 1016 to 1018."}, {"sourceSlug": "berber-mercenaries", "sourceName": "Berber Mercenaries", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "ali-ibn